The CaTiO 3  perovskite material was first discovered in the Ural Mountains and was named after the Russian mineralogist Lev Perovski. 25  At ambient conditions, CaTiO 3  crystallizes in an orthorhombic structure (Pnma space group). With increasing temperature, it undergoes a sequence of phase transitions from orthorhombic to tetragonal structure (I4/mcm) to approximately  T 1  ~ 1512 K, and subsequently cubic phase (Pm3m) at around  T 2  ~ 1635 K. 26  It exhibits a high dielectric constant ( ε r  ~ 170), low dielectric losses (tan  δ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
