A facile cold sintering method was successfully applied to a CaTiO 3 -Li 2 MoO 4  composite (5–25 wt.%) at 220°C for 1 h under 350 MPa. This approach enabled high relative densities in the range of ( ρ r  = 83.1–92.4%, Archimedes) and ( ρ r  = 79.1–90%, geometric), without any post-annealing treatment. The water-assisted Li 2 MoO 4  solution acts as a transient liquid phase during CSP, infiltrating and binding CaTiO 3  particles, and as a filler occupying residual porosity, thereby facilitating low-temperature densification of CaTiO 3  ceramics that are difficult to cold-sinter. X-ray crystallographic analysis of CSP specimens confirmed the presence of two primary phases, CaTiO 3  and Li 2 MoO 4 , in a two-phase system along with a secondary phase of CaMoO 4 , formed due to chemical interactions between Ca 2+  and MoO 4 2−  species in the liquid phase at a higher weight percentage of Li 2 MoO 4 . Vibrational characterization by both Raman and FTIR confirmed the coexistence of CaTiO 3  and Li 2 MoO 4 , thereby supporting enhanced densification. The SEM analysis revealed that increasing the Li 2 MoO 4  weight percentage fills interstitial voids between CaTiO 3  grains, as confirmed by EDX mapping, resulting in a well-packed microstructure. Also, TEM analysis revealed that the dissolution–precipitation behavior of the Li 2 MoO 4  in transient solution contributes to a significant densification. The densification mechanism is driven by a combination of localized dissolution, particle rearrangement under high pressure, and mass transport through a potential gradient, achieving high density despite the bulk insolubility of the CaTiO 3  particles. The resulting composites exhibit tunable microwave dielectric properties, with  ε r  = 48.7–39.7, tan  δ  = 9.1 × 10 −3  to 1.7 × 10 −3 ,  Q × f  = 466–2557 GHz, and  τ f \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
